EXT. PARKING GARAGE ROOFTOP - NIGHT

Sodium lights. Oil stains. One car left, a faded blue sedan with a flat front tire. The city glows beyond the low concrete wall.

FRANCINE OYELOWO, 40s, a dental hygienist's scrubs under a trench coat, kneels at the flat with a jack and a lug wrench. Her younger brother LEONARD "LENNY" OYELOWO, 30s, a rumpled courier's jacket, leans against the wall with a cigarette he hasn't lit.

She wrestles a lug nut. It doesn't budge.

LENNY
Want me to?

FRANCINE
No.

LENNY
It's been twenty minutes.

FRANCINE
Then it's been twenty minutes.

He pockets the cigarette. Watches. She grunts, adjusts her grip, tries again.

LENNY
Fran.

FRANCINE
Don't.

LENNY
I didn't say anything.

FRANCINE
You said "Fran" like you were going to say something.

He lets that sit. Wind carries the faint sound of a siren from the street.

LENNY
Mom called.

She stops. The wrench hangs from her hand.

FRANCINE
When?

LENNY
This morning. She asked if you were coming Sunday.

FRANCINE
And what did you say?

LENNY
I said I'd ask.

FRANCINE
Why would you ask me?

LENNY
Because you won't answer her.

She resumes. Harder. The wrench scrapes. The lug nut turns half a rotation.

FRANCINE
She's been telling people I'm "away on business."

LENNY
It's easier than saying you left.

FRANCINE
I didn't leave. I moved.

LENNY
Four hundred miles is leaving.

She rocks back on her heels. Looks up at him. Her face is wet, but her voice is level.

FRANCINE
You want to talk about leaving? You sent a postcard for two years.

LENNY
It was a good postcard.

FRANCINE
It said, "Weather's nice."

LENNY
It was.

A brittle laugh escapes her. He allows himself a half smile. Then it fades.

LENNY (CONT'D)
I should've called.

FRANCINE
Yes.

LENNY
I should've come.

FRANCINE
Yes.

LENNY
I'm here now.

FRANCINE
You're here because my car broke down.

LENNY
No. I'm here because I got the text.

She stares. He takes the wrench from her hand, kneels, fits it on the lug nut. Braces. Leans his full weight.

It gives with a crack.

FRANCINE
(quietly)
Show-off.

LENNY
Always.

He keeps going. Four nuts, a flat, the spare. She hands him each tool before he asks. They work in silence for a long minute, side by side, in the rhythm of two people who learned this from the same father.

FRANCINE
Dad hated the way you held the wrench.

LENNY
Dad hated everything I held.

FRANCINE
That isn't true.

LENNY
He hated the way I held you. When you cried.

She freezes.

FRANCINE
You remember that?

LENNY
Every day.

She turns away, looks out over the wall at the lit city. Her shoulders shake once. He doesn't touch her. He keeps working.

FRANCINE
Why now?

LENNY
Because Mom's sick, Fran.

The words hang. She does not turn.

FRANCINE
How sick?

LENNY
Sick.

She closes her eyes. Presses both hands against the cold concrete.

FRANCINE
You waited till the rooftop to tell me.

LENNY
You wouldn't have heard it anywhere else.

She nods. Slowly. A man's hand and a woman's hand finally touch on the wrench, and neither moves.

FRANCINE
Sunday.

LENNY
Sunday.

She takes the wrench. Finishes the last nut herself.
